.How this project is organised
[%collapsible]
====
The project follows the life cycle of the product. Foundation comes first, and then the development cycle repeats for each release.

[mermaid]
----
flowchart LR
    F[Foundation] --> D[Design]
    D --> I[Implementation]
    I --> V[Verification]
    V --> R[Releases]
    R --> O[Operations]
    O --> D
----

Each phase holds the practices of that stage. A practice (an internal control) tells what to do, and its evidence shows that it is done:

* *Foundation*: assign the security roles, and make sure that the team has the competence for them.
* *Design*: describe the security context, model the threats and the risks, and write the security requirements.
* *Implementation*: find security defects as the code is written, and document what is built.
* *Verification*: test the security, and check that the requirements are met.
* *Releases*: pass the release security gate, protect the integrity of the release, and document it.
* *Operations*: track the vulnerabilities and updates of third-party components, and keep the environments protected.

The practices are grouped in three adoption steps, so that you can start small:

* *Step 1*: the essential practices. Start here.
* *Step 2*: strengthened practices, when step 1 is in place.
* *Step 3*: advanced practices, to meet the standards.

The adoption step of the project is {{#field}}"field": "base/fieldTypes/adoptionStep", "cardKey": "{{this.project.key}}", "display": "inline"{{/field}}. Raise it when you have done the practices of the current step.
